    "documentation": {
      "format": "markdown",
      "content": "**Scientific study:** [dataset-study-telco-customer-churn on GitHub](https://github.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-telco-customer-churn)  \n**Dataset source:** [Telco Customer Churn on Kaggle](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/blastchar/telco-customer-churn)\n\nThis dataset presents a **binary-classification** problem built from customer-account records for a telecommunications provider. The study estimates the probability associated with `Churn = Yes` from service, contract, billing, payment, tenure, and charge information.\n\nThe analysis is predictive rather than causal. Model outputs and exploratory associations describe patterns in the observed data; they do not establish that changing an individual feature would change a customer's future behavior or validate a retention intervention.\n\n## Dataset overview\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---:|\n| Customer accounts | 7,043 |\n| Source columns | 21 |\n| Model features | 19 |\n| Identifier excluded from modeling | `customerID` |\n| Target | `Churn` |\n| Positive class | `Yes` |\n| Negative class | `No` |\n| Positive-class prevalence | 26.54% |\n| Problem type | Binary classification |\n| Selected model | HistGradientBoostingClassifier |\n| Primary model-selection metric | Average Precision |\n| Validation Average Precision | **0.6708** |\n| Final-test Average Precision | **0.6413** |\n| Final-test ROC-AUC | **0.8402** |\n\nEach row represents one customer account. The 19 predictive inputs cover customer profile, relationship tenure, phone and internet services, online services and support, contract terms, billing preferences, payment method, monthly charges, and accumulated charges.\n\n`customerID` is excluded from model inputs, while `Churn` is used exclusively as the prediction target.\n\n## Analytical question\n\nThe analysis asks whether the information recorded for a customer account contains predictive signal for distinguishing accounts with `Churn = Yes` from accounts with `Churn = No`.\n\nThe fitted classifier returns the probability assigned to the positive class. A classification threshold can then convert that probability into a binary predicted outcome.\n\nA feature can improve class discrimination without demonstrating that changing the feature would change future churn. Observed associations and model-based importance should therefore be interpreted as predictive evidence rather than causal effects.\n\n## Dataset and target contract\n\n| Role | Columns |\n|---|---|\n| Identifier | `customerID` |\n| Target | `Churn` |\n| Numerical features | `tenure`, `MonthlyCharges`, `TotalCharges` |\n| Categorical features | 16 customer, service, contract, billing, and payment fields |\n| Positive target class | `Yes` |\n| Negative target class | `No` |\n\nThe identifier is prohibited from model training. The target is encoded only for supervised learning and is never admitted as a predictor.\n\n## Data quality and preparation\n\nThe source contains 7,043 unique customer accounts.\n\nThe principal source-quality issue is limited to **11 blank raw values in `TotalCharges`**. Every blank occurs for an account with `tenure == 0`.\n\nThe preparation rule is deterministic:\n\n- blank `TotalCharges` values for zero-tenure accounts are materialized as `0.0`;\n- no rows are removed;\n- no mean, median, mode, or learned imputation rule is introduced;\n- raw source data remains unchanged.\n\nThe prepared snapshot is divided using a stratified **70% / 15% / 15%** train-validation-test split with random seed `42`:\n\n| Partition | Rows | Role |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Train | 4,930 | Model search and cross-validation |\n| Validation | 1,056 | Candidate comparison and threshold analysis |\n| Final test | 1,057 | One-time final evaluation |\n\nThe final-test partition remains sealed throughout feature definition, model-family comparison, hyperparameter search, candidate selection, and threshold selection.\n\nPreprocessing is fitted inside each training fold. Categorical inputs use one-hot encoding with unknown categories ignored at transformation time. Numerical scaling is applied only to Logistic Regression and remains inside its pipeline.\n\n## Target distribution\n\n| Churn class | Observations | Share |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| `No` | 5,174 | 73.46% |\n| `Yes` | 1,869 | 26.54% |\n\nBecause `Churn = Yes` is the minority class, accuracy alone would provide an incomplete view of predictive quality.\n\n**Average Precision** is therefore the primary model-selection metric. ROC-AUC, Brier Score, Log Loss, precision, recall, F1, F2, and balanced accuracy provide complementary evidence.\n\n![Distribution of the Churn target classes](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-telco-customer-churn/main/docs/images/churn_target_class_distribution.png)\n\n## Key exploratory findings\n\n### Contract term is strongly associated with churn\n\nObserved churn rates differ substantially across contract categories:\n\n| Contract | Observed churn rate |\n|---|---:|\n| Month-to-month | 42.71% |\n| One year | 11.27% |\n| Two year | 2.83% |\n\n![Observed churn rate by contract category](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-telco-customer-churn/main/docs/images/contract_churn_rate_by_category.png)\n\nMonth-to-month accounts exhibit a substantially higher observed churn rate than accounts on longer contracts.\n\nThis relationship is analytically important, but it does not establish that assigning a longer contract would independently reduce churn.\n\n### Churn is concentrated in shorter observed relationships\n\nAcross tenure quantiles, observed churn decreases from approximately **58.4%** in the earliest-tenure group to approximately **3.5%** in the latest.\n\n![Observed churn rate by tenure quantile](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-telco-customer-churn/main/docs/images/tenure_churn_rate_by_quantile.png)\n\nTenure contains substantial predictive signal but is also mechanically related to how long an account has already remained active. It must therefore not be interpreted as a causal retention effect.\n\n### Service, support, and billing variables add predictive signal\n\nThe exploratory analysis identifies contract type, online security, technical support, internet service, and payment method among the strongest categorical associations with churn.\n\n![Ranking of categorical feature associations with churn](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-telco-customer-churn/main/docs/images/feature_to_target_categorical_association_ranking.png)\n\nAssociation strength describes statistical dependence within the observed sample. It does not establish causal direction.\n\n## Evaluation protocol\n\nThe study compares one prior-only baseline and four trainable model families under the same feature contract and validation policy:\n\n- DummyClassifier with prior probabilities;\n- Logistic Regression;\n- Decision Tree;\n- Random Forest;\n- HistGradientBoostingClassifier.\n\nCandidate searches use **five-fold stratified shuffled cross-validation** on the training partition with random seed `42`.\n\nAverage Precision is the refit and primary selection metric.\n\nA candidate must exceed the Dummy baseline by more than `0.01` validation Average Precision to remain eligible.\n\nA practical tie is declared when:\n\n1. validation Average Precision differs by no more than `0.01`; and\n2. the candidates' approximate cross-validation Average Precision intervals overlap.\n\nWhen a practical tie occurs, predefined tie-break criteria are applied in order rather than selecting a model retrospectively.\n\n## Model selection\n\n### Candidate comparison\n\n| Model | Search | CV AP mean ± std | Validation AP | Validation ROC-AUC | Validation Brier ↓ | Validation Log Loss ↓ | Result |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| HistGradientBoostingClassifier | RandomizedSearchCV | **0.6728 ± 0.0161** | **0.6708** | **0.8477** | **0.1332** | **0.4135** | **Selected** |\n| Logistic Regression | GridSearchCV | 0.6591 ± 0.0129 | 0.6688 | 0.8470 | 0.1339 | 0.4175 | Practical-tie finalist |\n| Random Forest | RandomizedSearchCV | 0.6659 ± 0.0123 | 0.6679 | 0.8475 | 0.1593 | 0.4784 | Candidate |\n| Decision Tree | GridSearchCV | 0.6194 ± 0.0234 | 0.6134 | 0.8161 | 0.1462 | 0.8819 | Candidate |\n| Dummy prior | No search | — | 0.2652 | 0.5000 | 0.1948 | — | Baseline |\n\nHistGradientBoostingClassifier and Logistic Regression satisfy the practical-tie rule on validation Average Precision.\n\nThe first decisive tie-break is **lower validation Brier Score**:\n\n| Finalist | Validation Brier Score ↓ |\n|---|---:|\n| HistGradientBoostingClassifier | **0.133203** |\n| Logistic Regression | 0.133898 |\n\nHistGradientBoostingClassifier is therefore selected without consulting the final-test partition.\n\n### Candidate search configuration and hyperparameters\n\nThe search spaces are fixed before validation evaluation.\n\n| Model | Search policy | Evaluated configurations | Hyperparameters evaluated |\n|---|---|---:|---|\n| Logistic Regression | GridSearchCV | 24 | `C={0.001,0.01,0.1,1,10,100}`; `penalty={l1,l2}`; `class_weight={None,balanced}`; fixed `solver=liblinear`, `max_iter=2000`, `random_state=42` |\n| Decision Tree | GridSearchCV | 48 | `criterion={gini,entropy}`; `max_depth={3,5,8,None}`; `min_samples_leaf={1,10,30}`; `class_weight={None,balanced}`; fixed `random_state=42` |\n| Random Forest | RandomizedSearchCV | 40 | `n_estimators={300,500,800}`; `max_depth={None,8,12,20}`; `min_samples_split={2,10,20}`; `min_samples_leaf={1,2,5,10}`; `max_features={sqrt,0.5,None}`; `class_weight={None,balanced,balanced_subsample}`; fixed `random_state=42` |\n| HistGradientBoosting | RandomizedSearchCV | 40 | `learning_rate={0.03,0.05,0.1,0.2}`; `max_iter={100,200,400}`; `max_leaf_nodes={7,15,31,63}`; `max_depth={None,3,5,8}`; `min_samples_leaf={10,20,40}`; `l2_regularization={0,0.01,0.1,1,10}`; fixed `random_state=42` |\n| Dummy prior | No search | 1 | `strategy=prior` |\n\nThe randomized searches use random seed `42`.\n\n### Selected model configuration\n\nThe selected HistGradientBoostingClassifier uses:\n\n| Hyperparameter | Selected value |\n|---|---:|\n| `learning_rate` | 0.03 |\n| `max_iter` | 200 |\n| `max_depth` | 3 |\n| `max_leaf_nodes` | 7 |\n| `min_samples_leaf` | 40 |\n| `l2_regularization` | 1.0 |\n| `random_state` | 42 |\n\nThe final fitted object is a complete scikit-learn `Pipeline` containing the preprocessing transformer and classifier, so inference does not depend on separately applied preprocessing.\n\n## Final holdout evaluation\n\nAfter model and hyperparameter selection are complete, the selected pipeline is trained once on **train + validation** data and evaluated once on the sealed final-test partition.\n\n| Metric | Validation | Final test | Test − validation |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Average Precision | 0.6708 | **0.6413** | -0.0295 |\n| ROC-AUC | 0.8477 | **0.8402** | -0.0076 |\n| Brier Score ↓ | 0.1332 | **0.1394** | +0.0062 |\n| Log Loss ↓ | 0.4135 | **0.4207** | +0.0072 |\n\nThe selected model retains useful ranking ability on the holdout. Average Precision decreases moderately, while ROC-AUC remains close to the validation estimate.\n\nThese results support generalization within the same **stratified random-snapshot evaluation contract**. They do not establish temporal, prospective, or production generalization.\n\n## Educational threshold trade-off\n\nModel selection is based on ranking and probability-quality metrics rather than on a business classification threshold.\n\nA separate threshold analysis is performed exclusively on the validation partition. The frozen educational threshold is:\n\n```text\n0.2577809673219062\n```\n\nIt is selected to satisfy a validation recall target of at least `0.80`. It is not presented as a business-optimal decision rule.\n\nOn the final-test partition:\n\n| Final-test result | Threshold 0.50 | Educational threshold 0.2578 |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Precision | 62.50% | 51.25% |\n| Recall | 49.82% | 80.43% |\n| F1 | 55.45% | 62.60% |\n| F2 | 51.93% | 72.20% |\n| Balanced accuracy | 69.50% | 76.36% |\n| True positives | 140 | 226 |\n| False negatives | 141 | 55 |\n| False positives | 84 | 215 |\n| Predicted positives | 224 | 441 |\n\nLowering the threshold identifies **86 additional churn cases** but also creates **131 additional false positives**.\n\nThis makes the decision trade-off explicit. Selecting an operational threshold would require information not contained in the dataset, such as customer value, intervention cost, campaign capacity, false-positive cost, false-negative cost, and the expected effectiveness of a retention action.\n\n## Prediction and result interpretation\n\nFor each customer account, the model estimates:\n\n```text\nP(Churn = Yes | observed account characteristics)\n```\n\nThe probability is conditional on the supplied feature values and on patterns learned from the study sample.\n\nIt is not:\n\n- a causal explanation for churn;\n- a guarantee that churn will occur;\n- proof that a customer should receive a retention intervention;\n- evidence that changing an individual input would change the outcome;\n- an estimate of the effect of a retention action.\n\nA binary prediction additionally depends on the chosen probability threshold. Different thresholds can produce materially different precision-recall trade-offs without changing the underlying probability model.\n\n## What this study demonstrates\n\nThe dataset provides a compact but technically rich example of tabular binary classification with:\n\n- mixed numerical and categorical predictors;\n- moderate target imbalance;\n- explicit positive-class semantics;\n- deterministic data-quality treatment;\n- leakage-aware feature roles;\n- stratified train-validation-test evaluation;\n- fold-safe preprocessing;\n- comparison of linear, tree, ensemble, and gradient-boosting families;\n- explicit hyperparameter-search spaces;\n- a prior-only baseline;\n- practical-tie handling with predefined tie-break criteria;\n- threshold-independent and threshold-dependent evaluation;\n- a sealed one-time final holdout;\n- probability-based prediction;\n- explicit separation between predictive association and causal interpretation.\n\nIt also illustrates why model quality should not be reduced to a single score. Average Precision emphasizes retrieval of the minority positive class, ROC-AUC measures ranking discrimination across thresholds, Brier Score and Log Loss assess probability quality, and threshold-dependent metrics expose the consequences of a specific decision rule.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- The evaluation uses a stratified random snapshot rather than a temporal or prospective holdout.\n- External representativeness of the dataset is not established.\n- Future distribution stability has not been evaluated.\n- Production-time availability, latency, and consistency of all 19 inputs are unconfirmed.\n- The educational threshold is not a demonstrated business-optimal retention threshold.\n- False-positive and false-negative business costs are not available.\n- Customer value, campaign capacity, intervention cost, and expected economic benefit are not modeled.\n- The available evidence does not establish production-grade probability calibration.\n- No intervention-uplift study demonstrates that acting on predicted churn probability improves retention.\n- No subgroup fairness assessment is established.\n- Stability across populations or time periods has not been demonstrated.\n- No drift-monitoring or scheduled-retraining policy is evaluated.\n- Feature associations must not be interpreted as causal effects.\n- The study does not establish the safety or effectiveness of automated customer-retention decisions.\n\n## Responsible interpretation\n\nThe study demonstrates useful predictive discrimination for identifying accounts whose observed characteristics resemble patterns associated with `Churn = Yes`.\n\nSeveral trainable model families perform substantially above the prior-only baseline. HistGradientBoostingClassifier and Logistic Regression are practically tied on the primary scientific selection metric, and HistGradientBoostingClassifier is selected through the predefined Brier-score tie-break. The selected model achieves a final-test Average Precision of **0.6413** and ROC-AUC of **0.8402**.\n\nContract term, tenure, service configuration, support services, billing characteristics, and charges contain substantial predictive signal within this dataset. Neither the model nor the exploratory analysis demonstrates that changing these variables would cause churn probability to decrease.\n\nAny operational retention strategy would require additional prospective validation, feature-availability verification, cost-sensitive decision analysis, calibration assessment, fairness evaluation, distribution-shift monitoring, and evidence that interventions informed by the predictions produce beneficial outcomes.\n"
    }
  },
  "source_draft_schema_version": "1.0.0"
}